;; process-signal-vocabulary.wat — the naming target for the owner->child signal surface
;; (arc 278, DESIGN-STONE-process-signal-owner-to-child.md, ruled 2026-08-03).
;;
;; THREE THINGS NEED NAMES. They are weighed together because they appear together at one use site.
;;
;;   1. THE VERB      — an owner signals the child process it spawned.
;;   2. THE SIGNAL    — a CLOSED SET of what an owner may send  => an enum.
;;   3. THE OUTCOME   — a matchable value, never a raise        => an enum.
;;
;; ── WHAT THE VERB DOES (so the name can be honest about it) ────────────────────────────────────
;;
;; Given a Process the caller ALREADY HOLDS (it spawned the child), deliver a signal to that
;; child's process. Delivery routes through the kernel Pidfd the peer holds — NEVER kill(pid),
;; which our own clone.rs:215-216 documents as unsafe ("this PID may be reused").
;;
;;   - It is OWNER->CHILD. Holding the Process IS the capability; you cannot signal what you did
;;     not spawn. There is no :restricted-to gate — the value is the wall (ocap).
;;   - It is on Process, NOT Peer. Peer is the parent of Thread AND Process; a thread peer has
;;     no process to signal and the flags are process-global, so a Peer-typed verb would be PARTIAL
;;     (a domain hole named "the peer is a thread"). On Process it is TOTAL.
;;   - It is IMPURE (an effect on another process) and NOT total-in-the-fence sense — it returns an
;;     outcome because the child may already be gone.
;;
;; ── SIBLING VERBS it sits beside (the peer surface — anchors for register + the prime convention) ─
;;
;;   (:wat::kernel::send    peer msg)   -> SendOutcome
;;   (:wat::kernel::recv    peer)       -> (RecvOutcome :- [O])
;;   (:wat::kernel::connect addr)       -> (ConnectOutcome :- [S R])
;;   (:wat::kernel::accept  listener)   -> (AcceptOutcome :- [R S])
;;   (:wat::kernel::close   peer)       -> CloseOutcome
;;   (:wat::kernel::poll    peers)      -> (ServiceEvent :- [...])
;;
;; ✅ THE PRIME `'` QUESTION IS SETTLED — NO PRIME. And the way it was settled is the lesson.
;;
;;    The first draft of this target asserted "every peer verb above carries the prime" and asked
;;    the ward to weigh whether this one should. THAT PREMISE WAS FALSE ON DISK. Arc 278 `"0z"`
;;    (`70fe856d`, 2026-08-02 — THIS arc, the day before this cast) stripped the primes from the
;;    whole IPC/kernel surface: `wat-scripts/fixes/reclaim-ipc-prime-names.wat` is the recorded
;;    migration, and its table maps send'->send, recv'->recv, close'->close, connect'->connect,
;;    accept'->accept, poll'->poll, select'->select, spawn-program'->spawn-program, AND the types
;;    Peer'->Peer, Thread'->Thread, Process'->Process (lines 45-71).
;;
;;    Verified by own read: `":wat::kernel::send"` at runtime.rs:5717; ZERO primed hits across
;;    runtime.rs and check.rs.
;;
;;    WHERE THE FALSE PREMISE CAME FROM, recorded because it is the recurring trap: the primes were
;;    read out of COMMENTS (check.rs's must-use doc blocks still say "an unfaced `send'`") and out of
;;    the REALIZATIONS (R53/R55/R57, all written before "0z"). A comment is not the code, and a
;;    record written before a migration describes the world the migration ended.
;;
;;    So: a verb minted in the same arc that just finished removing the prime from every sibling
;;    does NOT reintroduce it. No prime.
;;
;; ── WHERE IT COULD LIVE (the namespace is part of the name) ────────────────────────────────────
;;
;;   :wat::kernel::  — where every peer verb above lives; the raw IPC surface.
;;   :wat::spawn::   — where spawn-program / ProcessOpts / ProcessLaunch live; the LAUNCH surface.
;;                     Note `:wat::spawn::process/post-spawn` already exists (a ProcessOpts builder),
;;                     so a `process/` segment here has precedent — but that precedent is for
;;                     OPTIONS BUILDERS, not for verbs acting on a live Process.
;;
;; ⚠ PRECEDENT CHECK, GROUNDED: there is currently NO verb anywhere in wat/*.wat that acts on a
;; live Process/Peer VALUE by that noun (only `:wat::kernel::` verbs taking a peer as an argument).
;; So there is no house style to inherit here — this cast is doing real work, not rubber-stamping.
;;
;; ── THE SIGNAL SET — CLOSED, AND IT NAMES WHAT THE HANDLER DOES ────────────────────────────────
;;
;; A wat program has NEVER thought in signal integers. Its model is the three user-signal predicates
;; and the stop predicate. So the variants should name the OBSERVABLE EFFECT IN WAT, not POSIX.
;;
;;   what we send   what the child's installed handler does      what the child then observes
;;   ------------   -----------------------------------------    ----------------------------
;;   SIGUSR1        substrate_on_sigusr1 -> KERNEL_SIGUSR1        (:wat::kernel::sigusr1?)  => true
;;   SIGUSR2        substrate_on_sigusr2 -> KERNEL_SIGUSR2        (:wat::kernel::sigusr2?)  => true
;;   SIGHUP         substrate_on_sighup  -> KERNEL_SIGHUP         (:wat::kernel::sighup?)   => true
;;   SIGINT         substrate_on_stop_signal -> KERNEL_STOPPED    (:wat::kernel::stopped?)  => true
;;   SIGTERM        substrate_on_stop_signal -> KERNEL_STOPPED    (:wat::kernel::stopped?)  => true
;;
;;   ^ FIVE signals, FOUR observations. SIGINT and SIGTERM are distinct on the wire and identical
;;     in what the child can see. That is the shape — see the ruling below.
;;
;; ★★ BUILDER'S RULING 2026-08-03, LOAD-BEARING FOR THE NAMING — and it was CORRECTED mid-cast.
;;
;;    First statement:  "we map SIG{INT,TERM} to stopped .... we treat them as equal in wat..."
;;    The correction:   "SIGINT and SIGTERM can be named.. independently... but they flow into
;;                       `stopped?`..."
;;
;;    ⛔ THE FIRST READING WAS WRONG AND IS WITHDRAWN. The apparatus read "equal in wat" as "one
;;    variant, and `Interrupt`/`Terminate` are disqualified names." That CONFLATED two different
;;    things: *these produce the same observation* is not *these are the same thing*. The shared
;;    (stopped?) is a HANDLER decision — both reach substrate_on_stop_signal — not an identity
;;    claim about the signals. SIGINT and SIGTERM are two distinct wire signals with two distinct
;;    numbers and two distinct conventional meanings.
;;
;;    ✅ SO: SIGINT and SIGTERM ARE NAMED INDEPENDENTLY. Two variants, both documented as flowing
;;    into (stopped?). `Interrupt` and `Terminate` are NOT disqualified — weigh them on their own
;;    merits like every other candidate.
;;
;;    AND NOTE WHY THE COLLAPSE WAS ALSO DISHONEST: a single `Stop` variant has to pick one signal
;;    to actually put on the wire, and then the form no longer tells the reader WHICH signal went
;;    out. An `strace`, a `ps`, a non-wat observer, or a future non-wat child all see the
;;    difference. Naming them separately keeps the wire faithful; documenting the shared landing
;;    keeps the wat-level collapse stated rather than hidden. Both axes honest.
;;
;;    ⚠ The tension this leaves is REAL and the cast should rule on it, not paper over it: two
;;    variants that produce an IDENTICAL observable in wat invite "which do I use?" with no
;;    wat-level answer. Does the naming carry that, or does it need the doc comment to carry it?
;;
;; NOT IN THE SET, deliberately — a separate question the cast should NOT try to settle:
;;   SIGKILL — the builder's open call (ChildHandle's Drop already sends it, handle.rs:138).
;;
;; ── THE OUTCOME — SHIPPED SIBLINGS ARE THE ANCHOR (do not invent a new shape) ──────────────────
;;
;;   :wat::kernel::SendOutcome         Sent | Closed | Lost[cause <- Failure]
;;   (:wat::kernel::RecvOutcome :- [O])      Message[..] | Closed | Lost[cause]
;;   :wat::kernel::CloseOutcome        Closed[exit <- (Option :- [i64])] | Signaled[signal <- i64] | Failed[cause]
;;   (:wat::kernel::ConnectOutcome :- [S R]) Connected[peer] | Refused[cause] | Rejected[cause] | Failed[cause]
;;
;; ⚠ NOTE THE TENSION worth a verdict: CloseOutcome ALREADY reports a signal, as a BARE i64
;; (`Signaled [signal <- i64]`, types.rs:1747). That is the RECEIVE side — "the child was killed by
;; signal N", where N may be any signal from anywhere, so an open i64 is honest there. This new verb
;; is the SEND side — a closed set of what WE may send, so an enum is honest here. Weigh whether two
;; different spellings of "a signal" across one substrate is a defensible asymmetry (open domain vs
;; closed domain) or an inconsistency that will read as sloppy at the use site.
;;
;; THE OUTCOME'S ARMS (the middle one is CONDITIONAL — STOP-2 in the stone requires proving ESRCH is
;; reachable through a pidfd before the arm is minted; an unreachable arm accumulates lies):
;;   delivered          — the signal reached the child
;;   already-gone       — the child had already exited (ESRCH); a REAL race the owner must face
;;   failed[cause]      — io failure
;; Must-never-happen stays a RAISE: EINVAL (unrepresentable — the enum forbids it), EBADF (our bug).

;; ── CANDIDATE NAMES — which one KEEPS ITS PROMISE at the use site? ─────────────────────────────
;;
;; THE VERB — the use site reads:  (<verb> proc <signal>)  where proc <- (Process :- [I O])
;;
;;   signal        — bare, obvious, reads as the noun-as-verb. Beside send/recv does it fit?
;;                   But `signal` is also a NOUN here (the 2nd argument IS a signal) — does the verb
;;                   colliding with its own argument's kind muddy the form?
;;   send-signal   — mirrors the Rust below (Pidfd::send_signal). Explicit, but `send` already means
;;                   "put a message on the peer's channel" — does `send-signal` read as a channel
;;                   send with a signal payload? That would be a lie.
;;   raise         — TAKEN. `:wat::kernel::raise!` is the language-level panic. Disqualified.
;;   kill          — POSIX-faithful and instantly obvious to a systems reader — but it says
;;                   TERMINATE, and three of the four signals do NOT terminate anything. A name that
;;                   describes a minority of its own domain.
;;   notify        — names the effect for the three user signals (the child observes a flag) but is
;;                   wrong for stop. Does the stop variant break it?
;;   interrupt     — same problem inverted, and collides with the SIGINT word the ruling just merged.
;;
;; THE SIGNAL ENUM TYPE:
;;   Signal            — bare, obvious; but is it too broad (it is NOT "any POSIX signal", it is the
;;                       four an owner may send)?
;;   OwnerSignal       — names WHO may send it; honest about the closed set, heavier at the use site.
;;   ProcessSignal     — names the target's kind.
;;   Notification      — names the effect, loses "signal" entirely; too soft for stop?
;;
;; THE SIGNAL ENUM VARIANTS (name the wat-level effect, per the ruling above):
;;   {User1, User2, Hangup, Stop}        — terse; `Stop` matches (stopped?) exactly.
;;   {Usr1, Usr2, Hup, Term}             — POSIX-abbreviated; rejected? (it re-imports the vocabulary
;;                                          the substrate spent arcs replacing, and `Term` reopens
;;                                          the INT/TERM distinction the ruling closed)
;;   {SigUsr1, SigUsr2, SigHup, Stop}    — matches the PREDICATE names (sigusr1?/sigusr2?/sighup?)
;;                                          the child uses to observe them — the strongest
;;                                          call/response pairing? But mixes registers with `Stop`.
;;   {Wake1, Wake2, Reload, Stop}        — names INTENT rather than mechanism (SIGHUP conventionally
;;                                          means reload). Honest, or presumptuous about what a user
;;                                          means by a user-signal?
;;
;; ⚠ THE STRONGEST CONSTRAINT ON THE VARIANT NAMES, weigh it explicitly: the child OBSERVES these
;; through `(sigusr1?)` / `(sigusr2?)` / `(sighup?)` / `(stopped?)`. The send-side name and the
;; observe-side predicate are two halves of ONE round trip, and a reader will hold both in view:
;;       owner:  (<verb> proc <variant>)
;;       child:  (:wat::kernel::sigusr1?)
;; Which variant naming makes that pairing self-evident WITHOUT renaming the shipped predicates?
;;
;; THE OUTCOME ENUM TYPE + VARIANTS (anchored on the four shipped siblings above):
;;   SignalOutcome     Delivered | Gone[cause] | Failed[cause]
;;   SignalOutcome     Sent      | Gone[cause] | Failed[cause]     ; `Sent` mirrors SendOutcome::Sent
;;   SignalOutcome     Delivered | Exited[cause] | Failed[cause]
;;   SignalOutcome     Delivered | Closed | Failed[cause]          ; `Closed` mirrors Send/RecvOutcome
;; Weigh: does reusing `Sent`/`Closed` from SendOutcome help (one vocabulary) or hurt (a signal is
;; not a message, and `Closed` means "the channel closed", not "the process exited")?

;; ── THE PROBE FORM — the name goes here; judge it at THIS use site ─────────────────────────────
;;
;; (:wat::core::defn :naming::PROBE
;;   [proc <- (:wat::kernel::Process :- [:wat::core::i64 :wat::core::i64])]
;;   -> :wat::kernel::SIGNAL-OUTCOME-NAME
;;   (:wat::core::match (<VERB-NAME> proc <SIGNAL-TYPE>::<USER1-VARIANT>)
;;     (<SIGNAL-OUTCOME>::<DELIVERED-VARIANT> nil)
;;     ((<SIGNAL-OUTCOME>::<GONE-VARIANT>   c) nil)
;;     ((<SIGNAL-OUTCOME>::<FAILED-VARIANT> c) nil)))
;;
;; And the round trip a reader holds in view, which is the real test of the naming:
;;
;;   owner side:  (<VERB-NAME> proc <SIGNAL-TYPE>::<USER1-VARIANT>)
;;   child side:  (:wat::kernel::sigusr1?)          ; SHIPPED — not renameable by this cast
